{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791463029,"nonce":"b/9hQdy5jq13csj/VtNx7IQO4Ek=","message":"Landed in 5444df3adc8dfccbe2be30331f0fb4d2b7f764a3.\n\nShared-HIR partial escape analysis keeps immutable, fully initialized objects in scalar fields through pointer phis. Opaque inputs retain their actual pointer and loads at the original access points. Calls materialize a virtual object only when all tracked pointer aliases are dead after the call; duplicate arguments share that one materialization. Writes through joined objects, identity observations other than null tests, incomplete/overlapping storage, unsupported pointer operations, and non-consuming escapes are rejected. Control-flow growth is bounded. Generated materializations are not reconsidered on later optimization runs. ZYNTAX_DISABLE_PARTIAL_ESCAPE=1 is a safe control.\n\nThe actual Lua operator HIR has no allocation on its typed loop paths: two allocations remain in the entry for persistent operands and two materialization sites in generic fallback paths. Static malloc sites are 7 -\u003e 4, eliminating the two executed temporary allocations per ordinary iteration. Constructor constants in otherwise-unused opaque-input scalar slots reduce the draft's phis from 153 to 108. Baseline had 26 phis. This remains general HIR work; no Lua frontend or benchmark-source special case was added.\n\nNUC, LLVM disabled, interpreter-first, frozen binaries, seven randomized repetitions per arm, affinity 0-7. Before is committed 7bc52ae96b622db080d3407c6b36ecaf0feb82de; disabled/enabled use the same new executable:\n- op_overload 10M wall: before 209.614 ms, disabled 214.700, enabled 96.169. Kernel: 177.844 / 182.078 / 66.997 ms.\n- op_overload 100K wall: 48.936 / 48.315 / 44.402 ms. Kernel: 18.087 / 18.397 / 15.217 ms.\n- Python nbody -n50 wall: 321.340 / 321.860 / 319.387 ms. Other Python and Lua controls are roughly flat; no broad Python performance improvement is claimed.\n\nSeparate CPU-0 comparison, seven repetitions: long operator wall 225.257 / 224.388 / 114.250 ms; LuaJIT 12.452 ms. Short: 57.020 / 56.198 / 46.542 ms; LuaJIT 2.090 ms. The LuaJIT gap remains. Both final timed sessions had stable executable hashes and no builds in 614 main plus 175 pinned monitor samples. Desktop/background activity is retained in the logs. One final Lua nbody enabled sample has a thermal flag; all samples are retained.\n\nARM64 final same-binary disabled/enabled medians: long operator wall 190.902 -\u003e 129.964 ms, kernel 155.423 -\u003e 97.088. Short wall 50.801 -\u003e 53.913 ms, kernel 15.930 -\u003e 17.630. A prior eleven-repeat constant-field comparison had short wall 48.006 -\u003e 45.983 ms; short results are mixed and not a cross-architecture success. A separate confirmation was stopped by its compiler-process guard and excluded. All raw records remain available.\n\nWhole-function native code still grows: 937 -\u003e 1165 VCode lines, stack reservation 464 -\u003e 624 bytes, static FP spill stores/reloads 12/12 -\u003e 15/24. These are static whole-function counts, not dynamic hot-path counts. Per-load virtual/backing guards and cold materialization state remain. Follow-up cdd2ecfa1c1f4fc65a5a6fc52ecd1f378dd8852a3e48a01fe12681b436709477 tracks guarded-read grouping and short-run cost.\n\nValidation on ARM64 and x86-64: 428 compiler unit tests, 10 heap-scalarization tests, 14 OSR tests, and 7 new partial-escape regressions. The new executable tests exercise interpreter, Cranelift and LLVM, including opaque mutable returns, duplicate aliases, null/zero-trip paths, pointer fields and pointees, re-optimization, and conservative rejection. The Linux LLVM test explicitly maps zyntax_alloc; its initial unresolved-symbol fixture failure is fixed. Lua43/Python50 conformance passed with LLVM off and on on both machines, excluding three official Lua suites. All 17 Lua and 22 supported Python benchmark checksums pass.\n\nConsolidated report: /tmp/zyntax-partial-escape-final.json. Final raw NUC results: /tmp/zyntax-partial-final-controls-nuc and /tmp/zyntax-partial-final-op-pinned-nuc. HIR/native audits: /tmp/zyntax-partial-final-audit-{normal,disabled}. Accepted NUC snapshot: /home/raikage/zyntax-perf-binaries/5444df3a. Source hashes match the commit. No push performed.","files":null}]}